---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import SignalToStructureField from '../../components/SignalToStructureField.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
	title="Technology"
	description="An introduction to neuromorphic technology, from materials and devices to algorithms, systems and applications."
>
	<section class="page-hero">
		<div class="container">
			<p class="eyebrow">Technology</p>
			<h1>A different way to sense, compute and adapt.</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Neuromorphic technology draws on principles found in nervous systems to build intelligent
				systems that operate through events, local dynamics and tightly coupled sensing and
				computation.
			</p>
			<p class="lead">
				Neuromorphic technology is strategically important for meeting the growing need for
				real-time intelligence in autonomous systems and equipment, complementing conventional
				approaches where solutions must be scalable and ultra-low-power, with low latency, high
				dynamic range, adaptive capabilities and, in some cases, biocompatible.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section tint">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">Foundational mechanisms</p>
					<h2>Four routes to more capable edge intelligence.</h2>
				</div>
				<p>
					The national studies identify four complementary mechanisms. Their value depends on the
					application, and the strongest results often come from combining them.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="card-grid two">
				<article class="card">
					<span class="number">01</span>
					<h3>Physical instantiation</h3>
					<p>Materials, devices and circuits can embody memory, filtering, recurrence and adaptation instead of recreating every operation in software.</p>
				</article>
				<article class="card">
					<span class="number">02</span>
					<h3>In-memory and near-sensor processing</h3>
					<p>Computation takes place close to stored or newly sensed information, reducing movement through the system.</p>
				</article>
				<article class="card">
					<span class="number">03</span>
					<h3>Event-based sparse processing</h3>
					<p>Changes and salient events trigger processing, allowing activity to follow information content rather than a fixed clock or frame rate.</p>
				</article>
				<article class="card">
					<span class="number">04</span>
					<h3>Biointegrated systems</h3>
					<p>Organic and electrochemical materials can bridge ionic biological signals and electronics for low-voltage sensing, adaptation and interfacing.</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="technology-intro">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">From signal to structure</p>
					<h2>Finding what matters in a noisy world.</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Real-world sensors produce dense, continuous and often noisy signals. Neuromorphic
						systems can exploit their temporal and spatial structure to encode informative change,
						update local state and support low-latency inference near the sensor.
					</p>
					<p>
						Digital computing remains essential. Neuromorphic approaches complement it by addressing
						applications where data movement, response time, continuous sensing, connectivity or power
						become limiting factors.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<SignalToStructureField />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">From foundation to deployment</p>
					<h2>Progress requires the whole stack.</h2>
				</div>
				<p>
					Neuromorphic systems cannot be developed effectively in isolated layers. Materials,
					circuits, algorithms, software and real-world requirements need to inform one another.
				</p>
			</div>
			<ol class="stack-list">
				<li><span>L1</span><div><h3>Materials and devices</h3><p>Organic electronics, memristors, spintronics, photonics, emerging memories and bio-compatible materials.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>L2</span><div><h3>Circuits and architectures</h3><p>Mixed-signal circuits, in-memory processing, event-based sensors and heterogeneous integration.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>L3</span><div><h3>Algorithms and software</h3><p>Spiking networks, event-driven learning, optimization, simulation, interoperability and benchmarking.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>L4</span><div><h3>Systems and applications</h3><p>Sensor fusion, autonomous systems, telecom, monitoring, healthcare and industrial deployment.</p></div></li>
				<li><span>+ </span><div><h3>Cross-cutting foundations</h3><p>Neuroscience, AI-driven codesign, ethics, regulation, security, humanities and social sciences.</p></div></li>
			</ol>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section compact dark">
		<div class="container caution-grid">
			<h2>Potential, with precision.</h2>
			<div>
				<p>
					Energy and latency benefits are application- and hardware-dependent. Neuromorphic Sweden
					aims to support careful benchmarking, realistic test environments and transparent
					comparison—not universal performance claims.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
	.technology-intro,
	.caution-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(20rem, 0.9fr);
		gap: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 8rem);
		align-items: start;
	}

	.technology-intro h2 { max-width: 40rem; }
	.technology-intro .prose p:first-child { color: var(--graphite, #222326); font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.7vw, 1.25rem); line-height: 1.62; }

	.stack-list {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
	}

	.stack-list li {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 4rem minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: 1.5rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
		padding-block: 1.5rem;
	}

	.stack-list span {
		color: var(--orange-600);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		font-weight: 850;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
	}

	.stack-list h3 {
		margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
	}

	.stack-list p {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.caution-grid h2 {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	@media (max-width: 52rem) {
		.technology-intro,
		.caution-grid {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
